// SOUNDNESS QUEUE row closure_capture_body_moved_never_dropped (tier 1).
// A `move` CLOSURE'S OWNED CAPTURE IS DROPPED BY NOBODY WHEN THE CLOSURE IS
// NEVER INVOKED — sema DELEGATES the drop to the closure BODY, and the body
// does not run.
//
// ROOT, by symbol: the §7.1 exception in `SemaChecker`'s closure-literal
// capture walk (src/compiler/sema_expr.cpp, the `is_move_type(move_check_t)`
// arm that fills `closure_owned_drop_` / `unskipped_captures`):
//
//     if (body_moved_outer.count(ec->captures[i])) continue;
//
// Its own comment says the body-side drop "is already the canonical drop site
// (callee fn-param drop, or body-local's scope-end drop)". THAT IS TRUE ONLY
// ON THE PATHS WHERE THE CLOSURE IS CALLED. For a NON-ESCAPING (stack-env)
// `move` closure the env merely borrows the source's storage, so mlir-gen
// emits no env glue — `MLIRGenImpl::emit_closure_env`'s `need_glue` is false —
// and `MLIRGenImpl::value_needs_drop` deliberately answers FALSE for
// `K::Closure` (its NOTE in src/compiler/mlir_gen_stmt.cpp), so the closure
// VALUE gets no scope-exit drop either. Sema skips the source-scope drop,
// codegen emits no closure-side drop, and the value is destroyed by nobody.
// MEASURED: `nm` finds NO `__closure_drop__` symbol in the object at all.
//
// ⚠ CONTRADICTS A RECORDED CLAIM IN THE SOURCES. That same NOTE says the
// closure drop is "driven NARROWLY: only the owning `Box<Closure>` path
// ... invokes gen_drop_value(Closure)". Measured on this binary, that path
// does not fire for a hand program either: `Box::new(move || …)` reads
// COUNT=0, and so does `Box<dyn Fn()>`.
//
// CONTROLS ALREADY CORRECT, bounding where a fix must not reach:
//   * THE SAME CLOSURE, CALLED ONCE — COUNT=1. The delegation is correct
//     exactly when the body runs, which is why the fix is not "always drop at
//     the source" but "delegate only to a drop site that is guaranteed to run".
//   * A `move` closure whose body only READS the capture (`s.len()`) — clean.
//     `body_moved_outer` does not contain it, so sema inserts it into
//     `closure_owned_drop_` and the SOURCE scope drops it. The mechanism the
//     defect bypasses is live and correct next door.
//   * The same value moved to a plain local with no closure — clean.
//
// THE CLASS HAS A SECOND ARM, three lines above this one and rowed separately
// as closure_narrow_capture_never_dropped: `if (narrow_owned) continue;`
// delegates an RFC-2229 narrow field capture to "the closure drop-glue", which
// for a non-escaping closure is likewise never emitted. ONE predicate — a
// delegation is valid only if the site it names is guaranteed to run — closes
// both.
//
// Found by tests/lattice/valgrind/sweep.sh; corpus member
// tests/logos/pass/rfc2229_move_disjoint_field.logos (2 allocs / 1 free,
// 4 bytes definitely lost, GREEN through run_test.sh today).
// Rust: exit 0, the capture is dropped when the closure is.
//
// ⚠ THE ORACLE IS A DESTRUCTOR COUNT, NOT valgrind and not an exit code alone.
// `D`'s destructor adds its weight through a raw `*mut i64`. Today the counter
// reads 0 and this program exits 1; correct is 1 and exit 0.
package closure_capture_body_moved_never_dropped;

struct D { v: i64, c: *mut i64 }
impl Drop for D { fn drop(self: &mut D) { unsafe { *self.c = *self.c + self.v; } } }

fn main() -> i32 {
    let mut n: i64 = 0i64;
    let p: *mut i64 = &mut n;
    {
        let d: D = D { v: 1i64, c: p };
        let c = move || { let _t: D = d; };
    }
    let got: i64 = unsafe { n };
    if got != 1i64 { return 1i32; }
    return 0i32;
}
